yours is not paying you  better than anything else you own  something is wrong with landlords  choosing profit over prejudice over time  the racial covenants unraveled in 1912  building owner Reginald shank told the  New York Times that he rented his  brownstone on a hundred and thirtieth  Street to Black's because I can get more  from Negroes than from white tenants the  same year an Aleve sold her building on  136 to a black family in violation of  the racial covenant on the grounds that  she had a right to sell to any person  she saw fit in 1913 a widowed landlord  on 137th was sued by her next-door  neighbor for welcoming blacks to her  building before the judge could issue an  injunction the neighbor dropped the case  and joined her  a hundred years since Payton's death  there no monuments honoring him or even  a plaque on his former home yet black  Harlem might not exist if it weren't for  this visionary realtor who saw that in a  free market bigots can be forced to pay  a price for their prejudice by making  the color line costly  [Music]
 

